      "type": "ai",
      "data": {
        "content": "Let me analyze this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand the clinical presentation:\n- 5-year-old child\n- Sudden respiratory distress\n- Soft tissue oedema with crepitus over the chest and neck\n\nThe key finding here is \"crepitus\" - this is the crackling sensation felt when gas is present in subcutaneous tissues, which is characteristic of subcutaneous emphysema. Crepitus over the chest and neck specifically suggests air has tracked from the thoracic cavity into the soft tissues.\n\nNow, looking at the chest X-ray image:\n- I can see increased radiolucency (dark areas) in the soft tissues of the neck and upper chest region\n- There appears to be air outlining the mediastinal structures (the \"spinnaker sail\" sign or \"continuous diaphragm\" sign may be present)\n- The lung fields appear relatively clear without significant consolidation or effusion\n- No obvious rib fractures visible\n\nThe combination of:\n1. Clinical crepitus (subcutaneous emphysema)\n2. Sudden respiratory distress in a young child\n3. X-ray findings of air in soft tissues and mediastinum\n\nStrongly points to **subcutaneous emphysema and pneumomediastinum**.\n\nLet me verify this with the tools to confirm my visual assessment:\n\n",
